That is the exact mechanism, read straight from the source, not inferred. Line 263 in `check.rs`:

```rust
if pairs.iter().any(|&(f, j)| self.transforms_between(f, j)) {
    ClosureState::Closed(pairs.len())
} else {
    ClosureState::Identity
}
```

The `n` in `Closed(n)` is `pairs.len()`, the total count of every fork/fuse pair in the graph. The condition gating it is `.any(...)`, an existential test: does at least one pair transform. So the reading is not "N pairs transform." It is "at least one pair transforms, and there are N pairs total." Every experiment matches this exactly: pure identity words stay Identity regardless of pair count, and the instant one real pair joins the graph, the report jumps to the full pair count, whether that count is two, three, or four, independent of how many of them actually did work.

Let me confirm the count really is unconditional total, not something coincidentally equal in my tests, with an asymmetric case.

